@node	4.3.3, 4.4, 4.2, 4.3
@subsection Implementing the @code{Amb} Evaluator

The evaluation of an ordinary Scheme expression may return a value, may never
terminate, or may signal an error.  In nondeterministic Scheme the evaluation
of an expression may in addition result in the discovery of a dead end, in
which case evaluation must backtrack to a previous choice point.  The
interpretation of nondeterministic Scheme is complicated by this extra case.

We will construct the @code{amb} evaluator for nondeterministic Scheme by
modifying the analyzing evaluator of @ref{4.1.7}.@footnote{We chose to
implement the lazy evaluator in @ref{4.2} as a modification of the
ordinary metacircular evaluator of @ref{4.1.1}.  In contrast, we will
base the @code{amb} evaluator on the analyzing evaluator of 
@ref{4.1.7}, because the execution procedures in that evaluator provide a
convenient framework for implementing backtracking.}  As in the analyzing
evaluator, evaluation of an expression is accomplished by calling an execution
procedure produced by analysis of that expression.  The difference between the
interpretation of ordinary Scheme and the interpretation of nondeterministic
Scheme will be entirely in the execution procedures, and in the engine that
drives them: this host has no re-entrant continuations, so instead of calling a
failure continuation in place, a dead end throws a @code{Fail} signal that
unwinds the branch, and the engine resumes the deepest pending frame from its
choice stack.

@subsubheading Execution procedures and continuations

Recall that the execution procedures for the ordinary evaluator take one
argument: the environment of execution.  In the book's design, the execution
procedures in the @code{amb} evaluator take three arguments: the environment,
and two procedures called @newterm{continuation procedures}.  This host cannot
re-enter a suspended continuation, so the execution procedures here take two
arguments: the environment, and a procedure called the
@newterm{success continuation}.  The evaluation of an expression will finish by
calling the success continuation with the value it produced, or by signaling
the @code{Fail} signal---the role the second continuation procedure, the
@newterm{failure continuation}, plays in the book's design---if the evaluation
results in the discovery of a dead end.  Constructing appropriate success
continuations, and signaling failures, is the mechanism by which the
nondeterministic evaluator implements backtracking.

It is the job of the success continuation to receive a value and proceed with
the computation.  If the use of that value leads to a dead end, the @code{Fail}
signal unwinds the branch; the frames that the branch pushed on the choice
stack are exactly the places the search can resume.

It is the job of a failure to try another branch of the nondeterministic
process.  The essence of the nondeterministic language is in the fact that
expressions may represent choices among alternatives.  The evaluation of such
an expression must proceed with one of the indicated alternative choices, even
though it is not known in advance which choices will lead to acceptable
results.  To deal with this, the evaluator picks one of the alternatives and
passes this value to the success continuation, leaving a frame on the choice
stack that can deliver the remaining alternatives.

A failure is signaled during evaluation when a user program explicitly rejects
the current line of attack (for example, a call to @code{require} may result in
execution of @code{(amb)}, an expression that always fails---see 
@ref{4.3.1}).  The @code{Fail} signal unwinds to the drive loop, which resumes
the deepest pending choice frame.  If there are no more alternatives to be
considered at that frame, the loop moves on to the frame pushed by an earlier
choice point, and so on.  Failures are also signaled by the driver loop in
response to a @code{try-again} request, to find another value of the
expression.

In addition, if a side-effect operation (such as assignment to a variable)
occurs on a branch of the process resulting from a choice, it may be necessary,
when the process finds a dead end, to undo the side effect before making a new
choice.  This is accomplished by an undo trail: the assignment records the old
value on the trail, and the drive loop rolls the trail back to the mark of the
frame it is about to resume.

In summary, failures are constructed by

@itemize @bullet

@item
@code{amb} expressions---an exhausted choice frame fails outright, and an
empty @code{amb} fails immediately;

@item
the top-level driver---to report that the choices are exhausted when the user
types @code{try-again} and no frame is left.

@end itemize

@noindent
Failures are initiated only when a dead end is encountered.  This occurs

@itemize @bullet

@item
if the user program executes @code{(amb)};

@item
if the user types @code{try-again} at the top-level driver.

@end itemize

@noindent
Failures are also processed during backtracking:

@itemize @bullet

@item
When the drive loop resumes a choice frame, it first rolls the undo trail back
to that frame's mark, so a @code{set!} performed inside an abandoned branch is
undone before the branch's alternative runs.

@item
When the failure continuation for an @code{amb} runs out of choices, the frame
fails too, propagating the failure back to the previous choice point or to the
top level.

@end itemize

@subsubheading Structure of the evaluator

The syntax- and data-representation procedures for the @code{amb} evaluator,
and also the basic @code{analyze} procedure, are identical to those in the
evaluator of @ref{4.1.7}, except for the fact that the dispatch must pass
applications whose head names a reserved form to the @code{amb} clause; the
book's tagged-list recognition becomes a check of the application
head.@footnote{We assume that the evaluator supports @code{let} (see @ref{Exercise 4.22}), which we have
used in our nondeterministic programs.}

@example kotlin
protected open fun reservedClause(expr: AppE): AmbExec? @{
    val head = expr.operator as? VarE ?: return null
    return when (head.name) @{
        "amb" -> analyzedAmb(analyzedOperands(expr))
        else -> null
    @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
Exercise extensions widen this clause with their own reserved heads
(@code{ramb}, @code{permanent-set!}, @code{if-fail}, and, in
@ref{Exercise 4.54}, @code{require}).

The top-level procedure @code{ambeval} (similar to the version of @code{eval}
given in @ref{4.1.7}) analyzes the given expression and applies
the resulting execution procedure to the given environment and
success continuation:

@example kotlin
public typealias Succeed = context(Raise<SchemeError>) (Value) -> Unit

public typealias AmbExec = context(Raise<SchemeError>) (Env, Succeed) -> Unit
@end example

@noindent
A success continuation is a procedure of one argument: the value just
obtained.  A failure is no longer a procedure in the chain but the @code{Fail}
signal.  So the general form of an execution procedure is

@example
(lambda (env succeed)
  ;; succeed is (lambda (value) ...)
  ;; a dead end throws Fail
  …)
@end example

@noindent
For example, executing

@example kotlin
ambDriver(::AmbEvaluator, prelude).solve("(amb 1 2 3)")
@end example

@noindent
will attempt to evaluate the given expression and will return either the
expression's first value (if the evaluation succeeds) or @code{null} (if the
evaluation fails).  The calls the driver makes in its protocol rounds use the
same continuations to deliver answers and to support the @code{try-again}
request.

@subsubheading Simple expressions

The execution procedures for the simplest kinds of expressions are essentially
the same as those for the ordinary evaluator, except for the need to hand the
value to the success continuation:

@example kotlin
protected fun succeedWith(v: Value): AmbExec = @{ _, succeed -> succeed(v) @}

protected fun succeedVariable(name: String): AmbExec = @{ env, succeed ->
    succeed(env.lookup(name))
@}
@end example

@noindent
Definitions evaluate their value first and install it when the value arrives:

@example kotlin
return @{ env, succeed ->
    vproc(env) @{ value ->
        env.define(expr.name, value)
        succeed(VSym("ok"))
    @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
Notice that looking up a variable always `succeeds.'  If
the lookup fails to find the variable, it signals an error,
as usual.  Such a ``failure'' indicates a program bug---a reference to
an unbound variable; it is not an indication that we should try another
nondeterministic choice instead of the one that is currently being tried.

@subsubheading Conditionals and sequences

Conditionals are also handled in a similar way as in the ordinary evaluator.
The execution procedure generated for an @code{if} invokes the predicate
execution procedure @code{pproc} with a success continuation that checks
whether the predicate value is true and goes on to execute either the
consequent or the alternative.  If the execution of @code{pproc} fails, the
@code{Fail} signal unwinds, exactly as it would past any other pending
computation.

@example kotlin
protected fun analyzedIf(
    pproc: AmbExec,
    cproc: AmbExec,
    aproc: AmbExec,
): AmbExec = @{ env, succeed ->
    pproc(env) @{ predValue ->
        if (isTrue(predValue)) cproc(env, succeed) else aproc(env, succeed)
    @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
Sequences are handled in the same way as in the previous evaluator, except that
the composition of the execution procedures happens at analysis time: to
sequentially execute @code{a} and then @code{b}, we call @code{a} with a
success continuation that calls @code{b}.

@example kotlin
var combined: AmbExec = analyze(actions.last())
for (i in actions.size - 2 downTo 0) @{
    val earlier = analyze(actions[i])
    val later = combined
    combined = @{ env, succeed ->
        earlier(env) @{ later(env, succeed) @}
    @}
@}
@end example

@subsubheading Definitions and assignments

Definitions are another case where we must go to some trouble to manage the
success continuation, because it is necessary to evaluate the
definition-value expression before actually defining the new variable.

Assignments are more interesting.  This is the first place where we really use
the engine's bookkeeping, rather than just passing continuations around.  The
execution procedure for assignments first attempts to obtain the new value to
be assigned to the variable. If this evaluation fails, the assignment fails.

If the value computation succeeds, however, and we go on to make the
assignment, we must consider the possibility that this branch of the
computation might later fail, which will require us to backtrack out of the
assignment.  Thus, we must arrange to undo the assignment as part of the
backtracking process.@footnote{We
didn't worry about undoing definitions, since we can assume that internal
definitions are scanned out (@ref{4.1.6}).}

This is accomplished by the undo trail: the assignment saves the old value of
the variable together with the assignment itself on the trail before
proceeding, and the drive loop rolls the trail back to the mark of the frame it
is about to resume.  That is, a successful assignment commits a trail entry
that will intercept any subsequent unwinding of the branch; whatever failure
would otherwise abandon the branch first restores the old value.

@example kotlin
protected fun analyzedAssignment(
    name: String,
    vproc: AmbExec,
): AmbExec = @{ env, succeed ->
    vproc(env) @{ value ->
        val oldValue = env.lookup(name)
        env.set(name, value)
        trail.addLast(Undo(env, name, oldValue))
        succeed(VSym("ok"))
    @}
@}
@end example

@subsubheading Procedure applications

The execution procedure for applications contains no new ideas except for the
technical complexity of managing the continuations.  This complexity arises in
application analysis, due to the need to keep track of the success continuation
as we evaluate the operands.  We use a procedure that delivers the arguments
one at a time, rather than a simple
@code{map} as in the ordinary evaluator:

@example kotlin
private fun deliverArgs(
    aprocs: List<AmbExec>,
    index: Int,
    env: Env,
    collected: List<Value>,
    procedure: Value,
    succeed: Succeed,
) @{
    if (index >= aprocs.size) @{
        executeApplication(procedure, collected, succeed)
        return
    @}
    aprocs[index](env) @{ arg ->
        deliverArgs(aprocs, index + 1, env, collected + arg, procedure, succeed)
    @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
Notice how consuming the operand execution procedures and collecting the
resulting list of arguments is accomplished by calling each one with a success
continuation that recursively continues the delivery.  The collected list is
rebuilt for each branch, so a backtrack never sees an argument collected for an
abandoned branch.

The actual procedure application is performed in the same way as for the
ordinary evaluator, except for the need to manage the continuation:

@example kotlin
context(r: Raise<SchemeError>)
protected fun executeApplication(
    procedure: Value,
    arguments: List<Value>,
    succeed: Succeed,
) @{
    when (procedure) @{
        is VPrimitive -> succeed(callPrimitive(procedure, arguments))
        is VProc -> applyCompound(procedure, arguments, succeed)
        else -> r.raise(SchemeError.NotApplicable(procedure))
    @}
@}
@end example

@subsubheading Evaluating @code{amb} expressions

The @code{amb} special form is the key element in the nondeterministic
language.  Here we see the essence of the interpretation process and the reason
for the choice stack.  The execution procedure for @code{amb} counts one
choice, delivers the first alternative at once, and pushes a frame that holds
the remaining alternatives together with the environment, the success
continuation, and the trail mark.  When the branch dies, the drive loop pops
the deepest frame, rolls the trail back, and delivers the next alternative; an
empty @code{amb} fails outright.

@example kotlin
protected open fun analyzedAmb(alternatives: List<AmbExec>): AmbExec = @{ env, succeed ->
    choicesTaken++
    deliverChoice(visitOrder(alternatives), env, succeed)
@}

protected fun deliverChoice(
    alternatives: List<AmbExec>,
    env: Env,
    succeed: Succeed,
) @{
    if (alternatives.isEmpty()) @{
        throw AmbFail
    @}
    frames.addLast(ChoiceFrame(alternatives, 1, env, succeed, trail.size))
    alternatives[0](env, succeed)
@}
@end example

@subsubheading Driver loop

The driver loop for the @code{amb} evaluator is complex, due to the mechanism
that permits the user to try again in evaluating an expression.  A problem runs
to its first answer; the success continuation throws the answer and the pending
frames out to the drive loop, which retains the choice stack.  A
@code{try-again} request resumes the loop, which pops the deepest frame, rolls
its trail back, and delivers the next alternative; when no frame is left, the
problem is exhausted.  Any other input starts a new problem, clearing the
frames and the trail of the old one.

@example kotlin
context(r: Raise<SchemeError>)
internal fun startDrive(
    exec: AmbExec,
    env: Env,
): Value? = keepDriving(exec, env)

context(r: Raise<SchemeError>)
internal fun resumeDrive(): Value? = keepDriving(null, null)

private fun keepDriving(
    first: AmbExec?,
    firstEnv: Env?,
): Value? @{
    val sink: Succeed = @{ value -> throw AmbDeliver(value) @}
    var pending: AmbExec? = first
    var pendingEnv: Env? = firstEnv
    var pendingSucceed: Succeed = sink
    while (true) @{
        val exec = pending
        val env = pendingEnv
        if (exec != null && env != null) @{
            try @{
                exec(env, pendingSucceed)
            @} catch (deliver: AmbDeliver) @{
                return deliver.value
            @} catch (deadEnd: AmbFail) @{
                // the branch died; resume the deepest pending frame
            @}
            pending = null
        @}
        val frame = frames.removeLastOrNull() ?: return null
        unwindTrailTo(frame.mark)
        if (frame.index >= frame.alternatives.size) continue
        val alternative = frame.alternatives[frame.index]
        frame.index += 1
        if (frame.index < frame.alternatives.size) frames.addLast(frame)
        choicesTaken++
        backtracks++
        pending = alternative
        pendingEnv = frame.env
        pendingSucceed = frame.succeed
    @}
@}
@end example

